---
import MapContentLayout from '../components/multiple-maps/MapContentLayout.astro';
import '../styles/multiple-map-canvas.css';
---

<MapContentLayout title="Custom Map With Multiple Pins – Colors, Labels & Embeds" heading="Create a Custom Map With Multiple Pins" description="Design a readable multiple-pin map with color groups, numbered pins, labels, popups, clustering, and three basemap styles." intro="Choose marker styles based on what visitors need to distinguish or do. A venue map might use color for entrances and parking. A market trail can use numbers to suggest an order. A popup can hold the opening hours or website visitors need after they select a pin.">
  <div class="mm-reading">
    <a class="btn-primary" href="/multiple-location-map-generator/?example=nyc#location-editor">Customize an example map</a>
    <h2>Choose a Marker Style for Each Job</h2>
    <div class="mm-style-examples">
      <div><span class="mm-pin mm-pin--pin" style="background-color:#24693e" aria-hidden="true"></span><strong>Standard pin</strong><p>Mark public entrances at a campus. Put the building name in each title.</p></div>
      <div><span class="mm-pin mm-pin--number" style="background-color:#2461ae" aria-hidden="true">2</span><strong>Numbered pin</strong><p>Show the sequence of stops on a self-guided market walk.</p></div>
      <div><span class="mm-pin mm-pin--circle" style="background-color:#7540a0" aria-hidden="true"></span><strong>Circle marker</strong><p>Mark secondary facilities such as bicycle racks near a venue.</p></div>
    </div>
    <h2>Group Related Places With Color</h2>
    <p>Use the same color for locations that serve the same purpose. For a campus open day, use green for entrances, blue for talks, and purple for parking. Include those categories in the titles or nearby page text so each location is identifiable by name.</p>
    <h2>Keep Labels Short and Put Details in Popups</h2>
    <p>Start a title with a distinct name such as <strong>South entrance</strong>. In the description, add a practical note such as “Step-free access via the ramp on Grove Street.” Add a complete website URL for the visitor guide or venue hours. Enable labels when visitors need to compare a handful of named places at a glance.</p>
    <h2>Pick a Basemap and Set the Opening View</h2>
    <p>Liberty shows roads and neighborhoods. Bright makes parks and waterways stand out. Positron leaves more contrast for colorful pins, and Dark suits a dark page. Keep <strong>Fit markers on load</strong> enabled when visitors should see every location. Turn it off and set the default zoom if the first marker should be the starting point.</p>
    <h2>Manage Overlapping Markers</h2>
    <p>Use clustering when several locations occupy the same neighborhood. Visitors can expand a group to see the individual pins. For a handful of widely spaced locations, turn off clustering and test whether the overall view stays legible.</p>
    <h2>Embed the Finished Map</h2>
    <p>Save the map, copy its iframe, and test it in a narrow page column. If labels overlap on a phone, turn off labels and keep the popup titles. Save the changes with the same recovery file so the iframe keeps its public URL.</p>
  </div>
</MapContentLayout>
